---
description: Use when building or reviewing UI markup and styling for accessibility, responsiveness, and interaction-state coverage. Do not use for Vue reactivity/composition specifics (see vue-component-patterns.mdc) or token values themselves (see design-tokens.mdc).
globs:
  - "app/components/**/*.vue"
  - "app/layouts/**/*.vue"
  - "app/pages/**/*.vue"
  - "app/assets/styles/**/*.css"
alwaysApply: false
---

DeployLab's own brief is "vivid interfaces without trading away SEO,
accessibility, or Core Web Vitals" — this rule is what makes that concrete.

- Start from semantic, keyboard-operable, labelled markup (`button`, `a`,
  `nav`, headings in document order) before reaching for ARIA; ARIA fills a
  genuine gap, it does not replace the right element.
- Build responsive by construction, mobile-first: prefer fluid `clamp()`
  type/space and intrinsic Grid/Flexbox layouts that reflow without a
  breakpoint, matching the pattern already used in `app/pages/index.vue`. Add
  an explicit breakpoint only where the design genuinely changes shape.
- Drive colour, spacing, type scale, and radii from
  `app/assets/styles/tokens.css` (see `design-tokens.mdc`); never hardcode a
  value the token system already expresses.
- Cover loading, empty, error, disabled, and focus states for an interactive
  component, not only the happy path; gate motion behind
  `prefers-reduced-motion`, matching the existing hero fallback pattern.
- Meet WCAG contrast and never convey meaning by colour alone; treat
  accessibility as a requirement built in from the start, not a pass at the
  end.
